WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.402
Cross joins make cardinality very easy to see because the multiplication is direct.

00:00:05.402 --> 00:00:09.582
Two rows crossed with three rows produce six result rows.

00:00:09.582 --> 00:00:13.312
One hundred crossed with one hundred produces ten thousand.

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:17.802
Ten thousand crossed with ten thousand produces one hundred million.

00:00:17.802 --> 00:00:25.197
That same multiplication can happen in ordinary joins when several rows on each side share the same key.

00:00:25.197 --> 00:00:28.477
The difference is that it may be less obvious.

00:00:28.477 --> 00:00:32.897
Imagine a customer has three orders and four support tickets.

00:00:32.897 --> 00:00:38.317
If you join both detail tables directly to the customer by customer_id,

00:00:38.317 --> 00:00:42.457
you can create twelve order-ticket combinations for that customer.

00:00:42.457 --> 00:00:46.502
Those twelve rows do not mean twelve new business events.

00:00:46.502 --> 00:00:49.642
They are combinations created by the join.

00:00:49.642 --> 00:00:56.432
Whenever your result becomes much larger than expected, do not immediately assume the database is wrong.

00:00:56.432 --> 00:00:59.567
Ask whether the join created combinations.

00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:03.467
Check how many matches each key has on both sides.

00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:09.542
Cardinality is one of the most important concepts in analytical SQL because it explains many surprising row

00:01:09.542 --> 00:01:10.720
counts.
